Expose the SCTP path MTU as a settable property of a live association.

libdatachannel accepts an MTU only through rtc::Configuration, applied once when
the SctpTransport opens its socket, and offers no way to change it afterwards.
usrsctp itself has no such restriction: SCTP_PEER_ADDR_PARAMS with
SPP_PMTUD_DISABLE and a zeroed address is handled by the assoc-wide branch of
sctp_usrreq.c, which writes net->mtu for every destination and calls
sctp_pathmtu_adjustment() when the value shrinks.

A11 needs it because usrsctp implements no path MTU discovery (usrsctp#205), so
libdatachannel falls back to RFC 8261's safe 1280 and every message is
fragmented to 1172-byte chunks whatever the path can carry -- worth about 3x at
64 KiB on loopback. Discovering the real value (RFC 8899) requires raising and
lowering it on an association that is already carrying traffic.

This adds, at each layer:

* setPathMtu       -- the association MTU, which governs message fragmentation
* setPacketCeiling -- only net->mtu, the ceiling on packet size
* sendPathMtuProbe / acknowledgedPathMtuProbe -- one padded HEARTBEAT probe and
                      the id of the last one answered

The split between the first two is what makes probing safe on a live association:
a probe needs the packet ceiling raised so an oversized packet can be emitted, and
needs fragmentation left alone so user messages are never split for a size the
path has not acknowledged. The probe itself needs the companion usrsctp patch,
which adds SCTP_SEND_PMTU_PROBE.

The header arithmetic is hoisted into a named constant so the call sites cannot
drift apart.

One unit trap worth naming, because it produced a silent false positive rather
than an error: sendPathMtuProbe takes *chunk space*, not a network MTU. The caller
has already subtracted the headers, and taking them off a second time here made
every probe test about 216 bytes less than the candidate it was meant to measure --
so a size above the path limit was acknowledged, applied, and stalled the stream.


